 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: $O(N \cdot S)$, where $N$ is the number of pixels and $S$ is the number of scattering samples per pixel. For multiple lights, it scales to $O(N \cdot S \cdot L)$, where $L$ is the number of lights [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(1)$ per pixel for the compute shader, plus storage for textures and constant buffers.
-*   **Performance**: High performance on GPU due to parallel pixel processing, but high sample counts and many lights can cause frame drops, with sources noting excessive drops when calculating visibility for 32 lights [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths**: Realistic volumetric light shafts, supports multiple spot lights, and operates as a post-processing pass.
-*   **Limitations**: Approximates single scattering only, visibility calculation is expensive with shadow maps, and direction artifacts occur if light vectors are not transformed correctly.
+Excessive frame drops occur when calculating visibility for 32 lights using shadow maps [S1, unofficial]. Not given in the sources: exact time/memory complexity formulas, measured performance metrics, or scaling laws.
 
 ## Background
-This method is derived from post-processing techniques for simulating volumetric scattering in real-time graphics, referencing Tomas Ohlsson's volumetric shader (`volumetric.frag`) and concepts from GPU Gems 3, Chapter 13 [S1, unofficial]. It was developed as a compute shader approach for DirectX 12 to handle multiple spot lights efficiently.
